Hi:

Try this:

n <- 60
dat <- data.frame(
    x = rnorm(n),
    y = rnorm(n),
    group1 = sample(c("A","B","C"), n, replace=TRUE),

    group2 = sample(c("L","M"), n, replace=TRUE) )

ggplot(data=dat, aes(x=x, y=y, shape = group1, fill=group2)) +
    geom_point() +
    scale_shape_manual(breaks = levels(dat$group1),
                       values = c(21, 24, 22),
                       legend = FALSE) +
    facet_grid(group1 ~ .)

HTH,
Dennis

On Mon, Oct 10, 2011 at 3:49 PM, Winston Chang <winsto...@gmail.com> wrote:
> I have a graph where my data is grouped by two variables. One of the
> variables is mapped to shape, and the other is mapped to the color fill. The
> variable that's mapped to shape is also used for faceting. Is there a way to
> remove the legend key for just that one variable? It is redundant in this
> case.
> In this example, the variable in question is "group1":
> library(ggplot2)
> n <- 60
> data <- data.frame(
>     x = rnorm(n),
>     y = rnorm(n)
>     group1 = sample(c("A","B","C"), n, replace=TRUE)
>     group2 = sample(c("L","M"), n, replace=TRUE) )
> ggplot(data=data, aes(x=x, y=y, shape=group1, fill=group2)) +
>     geom_point() +
>     scale_shape_manual(values=c(21,24,22)) +
>     facet_grid(group1 ~ .)
> Normally, to remove a legend key, you use opts(legend.position='none'), but
> in this case it removes both legends, which isn't desirable.
>

