Subject: Optimize pdf output size of geom_jitter
Message-ID: <F1aDyDcedzE.pzi2IadpneYJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ggplot2/c/F1aDyDcedzE/m/pzi2IadpneYJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello there.

I have a dataset with about 25.000 entries. I want to print a geom_boxplot and a geom_jitter.

This is the minimal example:
> library(ggplot2)
> library(tikzDevice)
>
> # The plot
> data <- data.frame(amount = sample(1:100000,25000,replace=T))
>
> q <- ggplot(data)
> q <- q + aes(x = '', y = amount)
> q <- q + geom_jitter(alpha=1/8, color='red')
> q <- q + geom_boxplot(alpha=1/100, color='black', outlier.size = 1)
> q <- q + coord_flip()
> q
>
> # Tikz -> PDF Output
> tikz('sample_jitter.tex', standAlone=T, width=5.9, height=1.0, sanitize=F)
> print(q)
> dev.off()
> system("export PATH=/usr/texbin:$PATH; latexmk --pdf -pdflatex='lualatex' sample_jitter.tex; latexmk -c",int=T)

Now I'm running this threw the tikzdevice and render an pdf with lualatex, so I still have a vector based graphic. Usually this results in a pdf file about 2 MB big. Once I reduce the size in Acrobat Pro it is only about 200 kB big.

BUT: All PDF-Viewer I tried so far (Acrobat, Apple Preview and Skim) have quite some trouble rendering that pdf (takes seconds), because it countains so many points. It even get's worse, when trying to print this pdf (takes minutes if not longer).

So my main question is (even thou, this is more a pdf than a ggplot2 question): How can I feather optimize the output to improve printing time?

Converting this to a png or jpeg is not an options, since I want the text to be sharp.

Greetings from Berlin
Alex

From uafe004a247e18cee at groups.google.com  Wed Oct 19 12:52:53 2011
From: uafe004a247e18cee at groups.google.com (dcl)
Date: Wed, 19 Oct 2011 12:52:53 +0000
Subject: Problem printing greyscale or B&W ggplot2 images
Message-ID: <U5SVjhzYGtk.70bT03Eo6mcJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ggplot2/c/U5SVjhzYGtk/m/70bT03Eo6mcJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I have an issue with printing/creating black and white and greyscale
images with ggplot2.

I have been trying to make some black and white, and greyscale
graphics for publication, they look perfect on mine and everyone's
monitors, but came out in colour when printed. (An awful photo of the
result is here: http://i.imgur.com/UuB6A.jpg)

For example:

-----------------------------------------------------------------
p <- qplot(displ, hwy, data = mpg)
p <- p + facet_wrap(~ cyl) + theme_bw()

 pdf("testplot.pdf",width=7.6,height=3.6)
     p
 dev.off()
-----------------------------------------------------------------

The generated image and output pdf looks perfect onscreen, but the
facets borders and titles are in a shade of red when printed. Try it!

I get the same results when outputting to a .png, and also when I
print directly from the R graphics window. According to my friend with
some experience with image manipulation, the images I've sent him have
bits of colour in them according to the colour histogram. (http://
i.imgur.com/v13dD.png <http://i.imgur.com/v13dD.png>)

Is there any way to generate true B&W and greyscale images using
ggplot? I tried forcing greyscale (perhaps very naively!) by using
'colormodel="gray"' in the pdf() command, but it does not make a
difference.

Any help would be greatly appreciated.

