Hi:

I'm afraid it may well be the only option. Last year, someone who goes
by the appellation 'learnR' released a 200+ page document on his blog
that showed the code for each graphic in the Lattice book in both
lattice and ggplot2. When it came to Figure 4.7, which illustrates a
dotplot reordered so that the counts were increasing in each panel,
the notation for the ggplot2 code was:
"Sorting each facet separately is not possible in ggplot2."

Unless Hadley or one of the package gurus demonstrates otherwise, I
think your surmise is correct. Here's what I hacked up last night, but
it lacks strip titles and a single x-axis title at the bottom; I'm not
sure offhand how one could add it once grid.arrange() does its thing,
but others on the list are capable of fixing the deficiencies in my
code below. One could probably hack the main function so that it uses
grid to return a plot with a text strip and then do some minor surgery
in grid on the result, but that's over my head at present. It's
applied to the data frame x2 that I created in my previous post. (BTW,
I'd be really interested in seeing how that's done.)

library('ggplot2')
library('gridExtra')

paretoChart <- function(d) {
   require('ggplot2')
   p1 <- ggplot(d, aes(x = reorder(reagent, freq, max), y = freq)) +
           geom_bar(stat = 'identity', width = 0.8, colour = 'black') +
           xlab("") + ylab("") +
           opts(axis.text.y = theme_text(size = 7.5)) +
           coord_flip()
   p1
   }

plist <- dlply(x2, .(comb_date), paretoChart)
do.call(grid.arrange, plist)

If you want the same frequency axis limits, you can add a ylim()
element in the paretoChart() function to fix the axis limits.

Personally, I find it useful to have some skill in both ggplot2 and
lattice; they often complement one another quite well. Occasionally,
one package drives you up the wall in frustration (for me, it's panel
functions in lattice that need to use subscripts) whereas the other is
straightforward. The latticeExtra package has also covered up a lot of
deficiencies in the lattice package that ggplot2 exposed and added
some nice functionality that ggplot2 is not yet ready to handle (e.g.,
the superimposition of two plots, which I've found handy a few times
recently). I like both packages and use both of them, although I
prefer ggplot2's defaults and have found it easier to understand with
more experience.

For this type of problem, the index.cond() function is the magic
incantation in lattice, which does not have an analogue in ggplot2
AFAIK.

BTW, the link to the aforementioned document is here:
http://learnr.wordpress.com/2009/08/26/ggplot2-version-of-figures-in-lattice-multivariate-data-visualization-with-r-final-part/

Look for the paragraph headed: 'pdf version of the posts', which give
you three download options. The post is a good read because it gives a
nice synopsis of the two packages.

HTH,
Dennis

PS: I also noticed a problem with the lattice solution in that the
bars did not start at zero. This could probably be fixed by removing
the usual axis padding on the horizontal axis, but offhand I don't
remember how to do that. If I find it, I'll let you know.

