I realize this is a very old thread, but I'd like to resurrect it, if only to disagree with Hadley's contention.  It can make perfect sense to plot binned data on a semi-log or log-log scale.  For one thing the binned data in a physics experiment can have an enormous range - you might have billions of counts in some bins and single digits (or zero) counts in other bins.  It doesn't make sense to look at data like that on a linear scale.  Also, physical processes will often give you a distribution of counts that is gaussian or exponential or power law in nature.  Those behaviors are obvious when plotted with the right sort of scale.  Sure, you can't plot log(0), but that's beside the point.

