On 10/29/2011 10:25 AM, Lorenzo Isella wrote:
> I also agree.
> Right, you cannot plot log(0), but it is common practice to bin
> log-distributed values. It is true that there is no way to define a zero
> level, but...it is not enough to give up entirely the log binning.
>
> Lorenzo

A histogram is both the binning and the representation of those bins
with bars.  It can make sense to bin data on a log scale, and then
represent the value of the bins with, say, points.  In the lingo of
ggplot, this would be a geom_point with a stat_bin (where geom_bar +
stat_bin = histogram).  I think it was the bar, not bin, aspect that was
problematic.

> On 10/29/2011 05:37 PM, David Morrison wrote:
>> I realize this is a very old thread, but I'd like to resurrect it, if
>> only to disagree with Hadley's contention. It can make perfect sense to
>> plot binned data on a semi-log or log-log scale. For one thing the
>> binned data in a physics experiment can have an enormous range - you
>> might have billions of counts in some bins and single digits (or zero)
>> counts in other bins. It doesn't make sense to look at data like that on
>> a linear scale. Also, physical processes will often give you a
>> distribution of counts that is gaussian or exponential or power law in
>> nature. Those behaviors are obvious when plotted with the right sort of
>> scale. Sure, you can't plot log(0), but that's beside the point.
>

